WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.990
Monitoring understanding means checking comprehension while reading, listening, practicing,

00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:04.274
or solving a problem.

00:00:04.274 --> 00:00:08.448
Instead of waiting until the end to discover that the thread was lost,

00:00:08.448 --> 00:00:10.374
you notice confusion as it appears.

00:00:10.374 --> 00:00:13.585
You pay attention to moments when the details stop connecting,

00:00:13.585 --> 00:00:17.116
when a term is unclear, when an assumption slips in unnoticed,

00:00:17.116 --> 00:00:19.043
or when progress is only superficial.

00:00:19.043 --> 00:00:22.895
This skill is especially important in complex work because errors can compound.

00:00:22.895 --> 00:00:27.069
A small misunderstanding in the first step can shape every decision that follows.

00:00:27.069 --> 00:00:31.564
In troubleshooting, a weak assumption can cause a person to collect the wrong evidence.

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:35.738
In writing, an unclear claim can make the rest of the argument unstable.

00:00:35.738 --> 00:00:38.949
Monitoring lets you adjust before the cost of correction grows.

00:00:38.949 --> 00:00:40.233
Good monitoring uses questions.

00:00:40.233 --> 00:00:42.159
What do I think this means?

00:00:42.159 --> 00:00:43.765
What evidence supports that interpretation?

00:00:43.765 --> 00:00:45.370
Which part is still uncertain?

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:46.975
What assumption am I making?

00:00:46.975 --> 00:00:49.544
Could I explain the last step without looking?

00:00:49.544 --> 00:00:52.754
What would I need to see to change my mind?

00:00:52.754 --> 00:00:56.928
These questions slow the work just enough to keep it aligned with reality.

00:00:56.928 --> 00:00:59.497
Monitoring does not mean stopping every few seconds.

00:00:59.497 --> 00:01:01.744
It means creating checkpoints at meaningful moments.

00:01:01.744 --> 00:01:04.634
Pause after a section, a decision, a failed attempt,

00:01:04.634 --> 00:01:06.560
or a new piece of evidence.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.808
If the material still makes sense, continue.

00:01:08.808 --> 00:01:12.339
If it does not, change the strategy: reread, draw a diagram,

00:01:12.339 --> 00:01:16.513
ask a better question, test the assumption, or seek review before errors harden.

00:01:16.513 --> 00:01:20.045
A useful signal is the phrase, I sort of get it.

00:01:20.045 --> 00:01:22.613
That may be true, but it deserves testing.

00:01:22.613 --> 00:01:26.145
Sort of understanding is often enough to follow someone else's explanation,

00:01:26.145 --> 00:01:28.071
but not enough to act independently.

00:01:28.071 --> 00:01:32.245
When that feeling appears, convert it into a concrete check: summarize the idea,

00:01:32.245 --> 00:01:36.740
apply it to a small case, or name the part that is still unclear.
